G3.d:
Ἀβαδδών (Heb. אֲבַדּוֹן, destruction; LXX, ἀπώλεια, only in Wisdom Lit., of the place of the ruined dead: Job.26:6, 28:22, 31:12, Ps.88:12, Pro.15:11*), indecl.; 
 in NT, Abaddon, the angel of the Abyss: Rev.9:11.†
 (AS)

G12.d:
ἄ-βυσσος, -ον (Ion. βυσσός = βυθός), 
  1. in cl., boundless, bottomless (e.g. ἄ. πέλαγος, ἄ. πλοῦτος Æsch.).
  2. [In LXX (for תְּהוֹם, Gen.1:2, al.; exc. Isa.44:27, Job.41:22 for צוּלָה, מְצוּלָה, Job.36:16 for רַחַב) and] NT, as subst. (MM, VGT, see word), ἡ ἄ. (sc. χώρα), the abyss; 
  (a) of the sea (Gen.1:2); 
  (b) of the underworld, as the abode of the dead: Rom.10:7 (a paraphrase of Deu.30:13 LXX); as the abode of demons, Luke.8:31, Rev.9:1, 2, 11, 11:7, 17:8, 20:1, 3 (Cremer, 2). 
 † (AS)

G15.d:
ἀγαθὀ-ποιέω, -ῶ (= cl. ἀγαθὸν ποιεῖν, εὐεργετεῖν), 
 [in LXX: Num.10:32, Jdg.17:13 A, Zep.1:12 (יטב hi.), Tob.12:13 B, 1Ma.11:33, 2Ma.1:2 * ;] 
to do good; 
  (a) univ.: 1 Pet.2:15, 20, 3:6, 17, 3Jo.11; 
  (b) for another's benefit: Mark.3:4 (T, ἀγαθὸν ποιῆσαι), Luke.6:9; 
  (with) accusative of person(s), Luke.6:33, 35 (Cremer, 8). 
 † (AS)

G27.d:
ἀγαπητός, -ή, -όν 
 (ἀγαπάω), [in LXX chiefly for יָתִיד, יָדִיד ;] 
beloved (see M, Pr., 221); 
  (a) by God: of Christ, Matt.3:17; of men, Rom.1:7; 
  (b) by Christians, of one another: 1 Cor.4:14; frequently as form of address, ib. 10:14; opposite to ἐχθρός, Rom.11:28 (see AR, Eph., 229; Cremer, 17; MM, VGT, see word).
 (AS)

G48.d:
ἁγνίζω (&gt; ἁγνός), 
 [in LXX always ceremonially, chiefly for קָדַשׁ ;] 
 to purify, cleanse from defilement; 
  (a) ceremonially: John.11:55, Acts.21:24, 26 24:18; 
  (b) morally: Jas.4:8, 1 Pet.1:22, 1 John.3:3.
 † SYN.: καθαρίζω, which see (and see: ἁγνός). (AS)

G166.d:
αἰώνιος, -ον (as usual in Attic), also -α, -ον. 2 Thess.2:16, Heb.9:12; 
 (αἰών), [in LXX chiefly for עוֹלָם ;] 
age-long, eternal, 
  (a) of that which is without either beginning or end: Rom.16:26, Heb.9:14; 
  (b) of that which is without beginning: Rom.16:25, 2 Tim.1:9, Titus.1:2; 
  (with) of that which is without end (MM, VGT, see word): σκηναί, Luke.16:9 οἰκία, 2 Cor.5:1; διαθήκη, Heb.13:20; εὐαγγέλιον, Rev.14:6; παράκλησις, 2 Thess.2:16; λύτρωσις,  Heb.9:12; κληρονομία, ib. 15; κόλασις, Matt.25:46; κρίμα, Heb.6:2; κρίσις, Mark.3:29; ὄλεθρον, 2 Thess.1:9; πῦρ, Matt.18:8; frequently with ζωή, which see 
 SYN.: ἀΐδιος, which see (AS)

G169.d:
ἀκάθαρτος, -ον 
 (ἀ- neg., καθαίρω), [in LXX chiefly for טָמֵא ;] 
unclean, impure; 
  (a) physically (LS, MM, VGT, see word);
  (b) ceremonially: Acts.10:14,28 11:8, 1 Cor.7:14, 2 Cor.6:17, Rev.18:2; 
  (with) morally: Eph.5:5, Rev.17:4; with πνεῦμα, as always in Gosp., Matt.10:1 12:43, Mark.1:23,26,27 3:11,30 5:2,8,13 6:7 7:25 9:25, Luke.4:33,36 6:18 8:29 9:42 11:24, Acts.5:16 8:7, Rev.16:13 (cf. Cremer, 320). 
 † (AS)

G266.d:
ἁμαρτία, -ας, ἡ 
 (ἁμαρτάνω, which see), [in LXX chiefly for חַטָּאת and cogn. forms, also for פֶּשַׁע ,עָוֹן, etc. ;] 
prop. a missing the mark; in cl. (see reff. to CR in MM, VGT, see word); 
  (a) guilt, sin (Plat., Arist., al.); 
  (b) more freq., from Æsch. down, a fault, failure. In NT (as LXX) always in ethical sense; 
  1. as a principle and quality of action, = τὸ ἁμαρτάνειν, a sinning, sin: Rom.5:12, 13 20; ὑφ᾽ ἁμαρτίαν εἶναι, Rom.3:9; ἐπιμένειν, Rom.6:1; ἀποθνήσκειν, νεκρὸν εἶναι τῇ ἁ., Rom.6:2, 11; τὴν ἀ. γινώσκειν, Rom.7:7; σῶμα τῆς ἁ., Rom.6:6; ἀπάτη τῆς ἁ., Heb.3:13; personified as a ruling principle, ἁ. βασιλεύει, κυριεύει, etc., Rom.5:21 6:12, 14 7:17, 20; δουλεύειν, Rom.6:6; δοῦλος τῆς ἁ., ib. 17; νόμος τῆς ἁ., Rom.7:23 8:2; δύναμις τῆς ἁ., 1 Cor.15:56 (cf. Gen.4:7). 
  2. As a generic term (disting. fr. the specific terms ἁμάρτημα, which see, etc.) for concrete wrongdoing, violation of the divine law, sin: John.8:46, Jas.1:15, al.; ποιεῖν (τὴν) ἁ., John.8:34, 2 Cor.11:7, 1 John.3:8; εἴχειν ἁ., John.9:41 15:22, 24 19:11, 1 John.1:8; in pl. ἁμαρτίαι, sin in the aggregate, 1 Thess.2:16 (see Milligan, in l.); ποιεῖν ἁμαρτίας, Jas.5:15; πλῆθος ἁμαρτιῶν, Jas.5:20, 1 Pet.4:8; ἄφεσις ἁμαρτιῶν, Matt.26:28, Mark.1:4, al.; ἐν ἁμαρτίαις εἶναι, 1 Cor.15:17; collectively, αἴρειν τὴν ἁ. τ. κόσμου, John.1:29; ἀποθνήσκειν ἐν τῇ ἁ., John.8:21. 
  3. = ἁμάρτημα, a sinful deed, a sin: Matt.12:31, Acts.7:60, 1 John.5:16. 
 SYN.: see: ἁμάρτημα. (AS)

G331.d:
ἀνάθεμα, -τος, τό
 (ἀνατίθημι), 
Hellenistic for Attic ἀνάθημα (Bl., § 27, 2); 
  1. prop. = τὸ ἀνατιθέμένον, that which is laid by to be kept, a votive offering (as ἀνάθημα in 2Ma.2:13, Luke.21:5—where LT read -θεμα, see M, Pr., 46). 
  2. [As equiv. in LXX for חֵרֶם,] devoted, a thing devoted to God (see Driver, De., 98 f., and cf. Lev.27:28-29), hence; 
  (a) of the sentence pronounced (Deu.13:15), a curse: Acts.23:14; 
  (b) of the object on which the curse is laid, accursed (Deu.7:26): Rom.9:3, 1 Cor.12:3 16:22, Gal.1:8-9 (see ICC on Ro.; Lft., Ga., ll. with; Cremer, 547; Tr., Syn., § v; MM, VGT, see word).†
 (AS)

G352.d:
άνα-κύπτω
 [in LXX: Job.10:15 (נָשָׂא רֹאשׁ), Dan LXX, Su 1:35 * ;] 
to lift oneself up; 
  (a) bodily; Luke.13:11, John.8:7, 10; 
  (b) mentally, to be elated: Luke.21:28 (cf. MM, VGT, see word).†
 (AS)

G386.d:
ἀνά-στασις, -εως, ή
 (ἀνίστημι), [in LXX: Zep.3:8 (קוּם), La 3:63 (קִימָה), קִימָה), Ps.66:1-20 title., Dan LXX 11:20, 2Ma.7:14 2Mac 12:43 * ;] 
  1. a raising up, awakening, rising (in Inscr. of the erection of a monument, see MM, VGT, see word): Luke.2:34. 
  2. a rising from the dead (see DCG, ii, 605b); 
  (a) of Christ: Acts.1:22 2:31 4:33 Rom.6:5, Phil.3:10, 1 Pet.3:21; ἐξ ἀ. νεκρῶν, Rom.1:4 (ICC, in l); ἐκ νεκρῶν, 1 Pet.1:3; 
  (b) of persons in OT hist. (e.g. 3Ki.17:17 ff.): Heb.11:35; 
  (with) of the general resurrection: Matt.22:23, 28 22:30 Mark.12:18, 23 Luke.20:27, 33 20:36, John.11:24, Acts.17:18 23:8 24:15, 2 Tim.2:18; ἀ. ἐκ νεκρῶν, Luke.20:35, Acts.4:2; τῶν νεκρῶν, Matt.22:31, Acts.17:32 23:6 24:21 26:23, 1 Cor.15:12-13 15:21 15:42, Heb.6:2; ἀ. ζωῆς, resurrection to life (cf. 2Ma.7:14, ἀ. εἰς ζωήν) and ἀ. τ. κρίσεως, τ. to judgment, John.5:29; ἀ. τ. δικαίων, Luke.14:14; κρείττων ἀ., Heb.11:35; on ἡ ἀ. ἡ πρώτη, Rev.20:5-8, see Swete, in l, Weste. on John.5:1-47, but see also Thayer, see word; by meton. of Christ as Author of ἀ., John.11:25 (see DB, iv, 231; Cremer, 307).†
 (AS)

G387.d:
ἀνα-στατόω, ῶ
 (ἀνάστατος, driven from home; ἀνίστημι), [in  LXX: Dan.7:23 (דּוּשׁ;* also in Aq., and in π. (see Deiss., LAE, 80 f.; MM, VGT, see word),] 
to stir up, excite, unsettle: with accusative; 
  (a) to tumult and sedition: Acts.17:6 21:38; 
  (b) by false teaching: Gal.5:12 (see Milligan, NTD, 73 f.).†
 (AS)

G467.d:
ἀντ-απο-δίδωμι
 (ἀντί ἀποδίδωμι), [in LXX for שָׁלַם pi., גָּמַל, שׁוּב hi., etc. ;] 
to give back as an equivalent, recompense, requital (the ἀντί ex­pressing the idea of full, complete return; see Lft., Notes, 46); 
  (a) in favourable sense: Luke.14:14, Rom.11:35, 1 Thess.3:9; 
  (b) in unfavourable sense: Rom.12:19, 2 Thess.1:6, Heb.10:30.†
 (AS)

G468.d:
ἀντ-από-δομα, -τος, τό
 (ἀνταποδίδωμι) [in LXX chiefly for גְּמוּל ;] ( = cl. -δοσις, which see) 
requital; 
  (a) in favourable sense: Luke.14:12; 
  (b) in unfavourable sense: Rom.11:9.†
 (AS)

G482.d:
ἀντι-λαμβάνω 
 [frequently in LXX for חָזַק hi., סָמַךְ, etc ;] 
to take instead of or in turn. Mid., with genitive, to take hold of; 
  (a) of persons, to help (see MM, VGT, see word): Luke.1:54, Acts.20:35; 
  (b) of things, to partake of: 1 Tim.6:2 (see Field, Notes, 210; Cremer, 386; and cf. συν-αντιλαμβάνω).†
 (AS)
